From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 05:09:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(credential-store): opt-in local key file cipher

The credential store encrypts only through AWS KMS or Vault Transit, so
on a trial or single-machine server with neither, every per-user
connection (GitHub, Databricks, MCP registry accounts) stays disabled.
The only way to try them is a throwaway Vault container, which some
hosting environments cannot run beside the server.

OMNIGENT_CREDENTIAL_CIPHER=local with OMNIGENT_CREDENTIAL_LOCAL_KEY_FILE
now selects LocalKeySecretCipher: AES-256-GCM under a 32-byte key the
operator creates and keeps in a file, with the row identity as the
associated data. The design says "there is no non-KMS fallback", and
this keeps it: the backend is an explicit choice, never a fallback.

- It is used only when the selector names it. The key file variable
  without the selector is a startup error, `local` beside a configured
  KMS or Vault key is refused, and with `kms` or `vault` selected the
  key file is ignored, so a failing KMS or Vault call still fails.
- The key file must exist, be a regular file with no access for group
  or others, and hold one 32-byte key in base64; anything else raises
  when the cipher is built, which stops startup. The server never
  generates, writes or logs a key; it logs the file's path.
- Ciphertext is `oslocal:v1:<key id>:<payload>`, apart from KMS's bare
  base64 and Vault's `osvault:` envelope. The key id is a fingerprint,
  so a different key raises store-wide, as the Vault backend does for a
  repointed key, instead of reading as a per-user disconnect. A wrong
  identity or an altered ciphertext reads as disconnected.

The trade-off is stated in designs/CREDENTIAL_STORE.md: anyone who can
read the key file and the database can read stored tokens, no decrypt
is logged or revocable, and changing the key means every user
reconnects.

No dependency changes: `cryptography` is already installed with the base
package through PyJWT[crypto] and imported directly elsewhere.

Not upstream yet: not proposed, and there is no contribution branch for
it. Upstream's design rules out a local key by default, so this would
need a maintainer's agreement first; no open issue or pull request asks
for one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
